# Job offers: work beside knitting (scripts/jobs.gd, data/economy.json "jobs"). The format is
# described at the top of scripts/story.gd. Every line here is a draft for Alex.
#
# These have no @trigger: jobs.gd plays "job_<id>" the first time Nora talks to the boss once the
# offer is due (after Act 1; Sabrina also waits for her trust to reach the job's "trust"). Taking
# the job is "set job <id>"; she holds one job at a time, so taking a new one leaves the old one.
# Either way "job_offer_<id>" is set, so a "Take the job" row stays on the boss's menu.

== job_bakery
@goal
@face giorgio nora
@face nora giorgio
@camera between giorgio nora 6
Giorgio (warm): Cara. Sit a minute. The oven doesn't need me for ten.
Giorgio (neutral): Rent on Ellie's place, a tin of coins, a flock that eats more than it grows. I know that arithmetic. I did it once myself, in another city.
> He doesn't say which city. He wipes the counter where it's already clean.
Giorgio (warm): So. I need hands at six in the morning. Four hours: the oven, the flour, the shelves. Forty-five coins a shift, and you don't go home hungry.
Nora (worried): I haven't worked anywhere with people in a long time. I'm not... good, in the mornings. With people.
Giorgio (warm): Bread doesn't ask questions. That's why I like it. You knead, it rises, nobody needs to talk.
? A steady wage, any morning she wants one.
- I'll take it. Six o'clock? | set job bakery; trust giorgio +2; familiarity giorgio +2
- Can I think about it? -> later | familiarity giorgio +1
Giorgio (happy): Six. Not five past. The dough knows.
@goto end
@label later
Giorgio (warm): Think as long as you like. The offer keeps better than my bread.
@label end
@set job_offer_bakery

== job_cafe
@face sabrina nora
@face nora sabrina
@camera between sabrina nora 6 yaw building_cafe
Sabrina (neutral): Okay, so. Don't make this weird.
Sabrina (wry): My aunt needs someone on the terrace after lunch. She asked if I knew anyone who isn't, quote, "a disaster."
Sabrina (neutral): You're the least disaster person I know. Which is a low bar. It's basically on the floor.
Nora (happy): Are you offering me a job?
Sabrina (wry): I'm relaying information. Afternoons, four hours, forty coins. You carry cups, you wipe tables, you don't ask about my feelings.
? Afternoons on the terrace with Sabrina.
- Deal. No feelings. | set job cafe; trust sabrina +2
- Let me think about it. -> later | familiarity sabrina +1
Sabrina (happy): Cool. I mean. Fine. Whatever. Don't be late.
@goto end
@label later
Sabrina (neutral): Sure. It's not like I'll be sitting here waiting. I'll literally be sitting here. But not waiting.
@label end
@set job_offer_cafe

== job_post
@face barbara nora
@face nora barbara
@camera between barbara nora 6
Barbara (warm): Sweetheart, walk with me a minute. My knees and I have been having words.
Barbara (neutral): Forty years I've carried this bag round Maple Grove. Either it's getting heavier or I'm getting honest.
Barbara (warm): I could use someone on the morning round. Every door in town, letters in, a kind word for whoever opens up. Three hours, thirty-eight coins, and all the fresh air you can breathe.
Nora (wry): I've walked out of a lot of doors lately. Mostly away from things.
Barbara (warm): Then you'll be good at this. A round always brings you back where you started. That's the comfort of it.
? Mornings on the post round.
- I'd like that. | set job post; trust barbara +2; familiarity barbara +2
- Let me think on it. -> later | familiarity barbara +1
Barbara (happy): Eight o'clock, by the fountain. Wear good shoes, sweetheart. The cobbles have opinions.
@goto end
@label later
Barbara (warm): No rush. The post comes every day, and so do I.
@label end
@set job_offer_post
